###Pure
// Where the executable corpus meets the project dependency graph.
//
// projects/ holds 56 Legend projects that depend on each other the way real ones do, and all
// 56 compile -- alone with their declared closure, and together. What none of them does is
// RUN: the contract forbade data elements, runtimes and services, so the graph proves that a
// cross-project reference COMPILES and nothing more.
//
// That is a real gap rather than a tidy boundary. Three of this session's findings -- F50 (a
// derived Boolean compared to a literal), F51 (isEmpty over a non-key join) and F53 (a REAL
// column) -- compile perfectly and fail at execution. A cross-project join that lowered to
// wrong SQL would be invisible to every check the graph currently has.
//
// So one project is pulled across the line. `core-tenor` is seeded, its mapping is included
// in stress::AllMapping, and the corpus navigates INTO it from a corpus-owned root. Two
// directions are worth having and they test different things:
//
//   * a service rooted at a PROJECT class, executing against seeded project data -- which is
//     the first time anything in projects/ has produced a row
//   * a service rooted at a CORPUS class that reaches a project attribute through an
//     association -- which is the question a user actually asks of a dependency, and the one
//     a compile check cannot answer
//
// core-tenor was chosen because its join is a RANGE. A key equality that lowers wrongly
// usually returns nothing and is obvious; a range that lowers wrongly returns the wrong
// BAND, which is a plausible number in the right shape. core-fx and core-ratings followed,
// for a cross-project FUNCTION CALL and for MILESTONING across the boundary.
//
// Note the set ids on the CORPUS side of each association below are the DEFAULT ones --
// `counterparty_Counterparty` -- because that mapping names no id. Every
// project in projects/ names its ids explicitly, so the two sides of one association here
// follow opposite conventions, and neither is guessable from the class name alone.
// A corpus counterparty reaching a MILESTONED class in another project. `all(%date)` on
// core_ratings::RatingVersion is a question projects/ could only ever compile -- the graph
// has no data and no runtime -- and the dated form is enforced rather than conventional, so
// the boundary either carries the temporal obligation across or it does not.
//
// Two of the five seeded counterparties are deliberately UNRATED, so a navigation from them
// lands on nothing. A seed where everything is rated would never show that.
Association projectlink::CounterpartyRatings
{
   ratedCounterparty: counterparty::Counterparty[0..1];
   ratingVersions: core_ratings::RatingVersion[*];
}

// A corpus pricing row reaching a project FX rate on the rate's FULL three-column key. The
// rate is only half of it: the point is that a corpus DERIVED PROPERTY calls core_fx's own
// function on what it finds, which is a cross-project function call at execution.
//
// The owner is projectlink::PricedTrade rather than trading::Trade, because the trade alone
// does not say which pair or which rate type to value it at -- and joining on currency alone
// is not to-one, which the oracle refused rather than silently picking a row.
Association projectlink::TradeFxRate
{
   pricedTrades: projectlink::PricedTrade[*];
   fxRate: core_fx::CfxRate[0..1];
}

// A CORPUS class whose derived property calls a PROJECT's function on a value reached
// across the boundary. Declared here rather than on trading::Trade itself, because a corpus
// class should not need editing to gain a dependency -- and because an extension class is
// what a project consumer would actually write.
Class projectlink::PricedTrade
{
   tradeId: String[1];
   pairCode: String[1];
   notional: Float[1];
   fxMid: Float[1];

   // The cross-project function call, at EXECUTION. `core_fx::convert` lives in another
   // project entirely; nothing in projects/ has ever run one.
   notionalConverted() { core_fx::convert($this.notional, $this.fxMid) } : Float[1];
   // And its inverse, so a wrong lowering shows up as a number that does not round-trip.
   // `$this.notionalConverted` without parentheses: a no-argument derived property is
   // referenced like a property, and the parenthesised form is not what this corpus
   // writes elsewhere.
   backConverted() { core_fx::convert($this.notionalConverted, core_fx::invert($this.fxMid)) } : Float[1];
}

Association projectlink::PillarTenorBucket
{
   // A curve pillar's tenor in days decides which bucket it falls in. The bucket table
   // belongs to core-tenor and the pillar to the corpus, so this association is the boundary.
   bucketedPillars: curves::CurvePoint[*];
   tenorBucket: core_tenor::CtnTenorBucket[0..1];
}

###Mapping
Mapping projectlink::ProjectLinkMapping
(
   include core_tenor::Mapping
   include core_fx::Mapping
   include core_ratings::Mapping
   include fee_core::Mapping
   include core_instrument::Mapping
   include core_calendar::Mapping
   include core_units::Mapping

   projectlink::CounterpartyRatings: Relational
   {
      AssociationMapping
      (
         ratingVersions[counterparty_Counterparty, crRatingVersion]: [store::DB]@Counterparty_RatingVersion,
         ratedCounterparty[crRatingVersion, counterparty_Counterparty]: [store::DB]@Counterparty_RatingVersion
      )
   }

   projectlink::TradeFxRate: Relational
   {
      AssociationMapping
      (
         fxRate[projectlinkPricedTrade, cfxRate]: [store::DB]@Trade_FxRate,
         pricedTrades[cfxRate, projectlinkPricedTrade]: [store::DB]@Trade_FxRate
      )
   }

   projectlink::PricedTrade[projectlinkPricedTrade]: Relational
   {
      ~primaryKey ( [store::DB]PROJECTLINK_TRADE_FX.TRADE_ID )
      ~mainTable [store::DB]PROJECTLINK_TRADE_FX
      tradeId: [store::DB]PROJECTLINK_TRADE_FX.TRADE_ID,
      pairCode: [store::DB]PROJECTLINK_TRADE_FX.PAIR_CODE,
      notional: [store::DB]@PricedTrade_Trade | [store::DB]TRADE.NOTIONAL,
      fxMid: [store::DB]@Trade_FxRate | [store::DB]CFX_RATE.MID
   }

   projectlink::PillarTenorBucket: Relational
   {
      AssociationMapping
      (
         // The far set id is core-tenor's own, read from its MANIFEST. It names its sets
         // explicitly, so `core_tenor_CtnTenorBucket` does not exist.
         tenorBucket[pillar, ctnBucket]: [store::DB]@Pillar_TenorBucket,
         bucketedPillars[ctnBucket, pillar]: [store::DB]@Pillar_TenorBucket
      )
   }
)
